        'The strategic significance exceeds the agronomic result. Senegal directs 93% of its water demand to agriculture, irrigates less than a third of its irrigable potential, and imports the fertilizer its soils consume. A demonstrated capacity to hold water and nutrient inside the root zone converts each of those constraints into recoverable margin — and does so with technology that is biodegradable, patent-protected, and entering industrial-scale production on a committed path to two continents by 2030.',
